<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aurel Stein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Marc Aurel Stein</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aurel Stein was a Hungarian-born British archaeologist and explorer who led four expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> between 1900 and 1930. He excavated sites along the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, including Niya, Loulan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>, where he acquired thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra">Diamond Sutra</a>, the oldest dated printed book. His finds, now mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> and British Library, transformed the study of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurel-stein.jpg" alt="Aurel Stein" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thompson, The Grosvenor Studios · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurel_Stein_1909.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marc Aurel Stein was born on 26 November 1862 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> to Náthán Stein and Anna Hirschler, a Jewish couple. His parents and sister kept their faith, but Stein and his brother Ernst Eduard were baptised as Lutherans. The family spoke German and Hungarian at home. He studied at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, graduating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Persian, and took his doctorate at Tübingen in 1883. In 1884 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to study oriental languages and archaeology, and in 1887 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where he became Registrar of the University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and then, from 1888 to 1899, Principal of the Oriental College at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sven-hedin">Sven Hedin</a>'s Through Asia, Stein sought the help of the Indologist Rudolf Hoernle in June 1898 to find and study Central Asian antiquities. With Hoernle's lobbying, the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> approved his proposal, and he received funds for his first expedition in January 1899. He became a British citizen in 1904. He made four major expeditions, in 1900 to 1901, 1906 to 1908, 1913 to 1916 and 1930. On the first he uncovered relics at the oasis of Dandan Oilik in the Taklamakan Desert, and on the third he excavated at Khara-Khoto. In 1901 he exposed the forgeries of Islam Akhun. On the second expedition he lost several toes to frostbite in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunlun-mountains">Kunlun Mountains</a>.</p><p>His most significant find came in 1907 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogao-caves">Mogao Caves</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>, where he found a printed copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra">Diamond Sutra</a> dated 868 and some 40,000 other scrolls. He gained the confidence of the Taoist caretaker, whom he also paid, and took away 24 cases of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and 4 of paintings and textiles. He was knighted, but Chinese nationalists called him a burglar and staged protests. His fourth expedition ended in failure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Stein was also an ethnographer, geographer, linguist and surveyor. He translated Kalhaṇa's Rājataraṅgiṇī, a chronicle of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, which was published in 1900, and wrote up his journeys in Ancient Khotan, Serindia and Innermost Asia. He found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in the Tocharian languages at Miran and recorded many sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a>. Between 1940 and 1943 he surveyed the Ghaggar-Hakra River and identified about 100 prehistoric and historical sites. His collections are divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, the British Library, the Srinagar Museum and the National Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>.</p><p>He received the Royal Geographical Society's Founder's Medal in 1909 and was made a Knight Commander of the Order of the Indian Empire in 1912. He never married, and was always accompanied by a dog named Dash, of which there were seven. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> on 26 October 1943 and is buried in the Sherpur Cantonment there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aurel Stein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their earliest surviving letters, the Ancient Letters, were found by <b>Aurel Stein</b> in 1907 in an abandoned watchtower near Dunhuang.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1920s and 1930s Marc <b>Aurel Stein</b> and others found thousands of scrolls in the Mogao Caves near Dunhuang, including more than 50 partial and complete manuscripts and a copy of the lost Xiang'er commentary.</blockquote><a href="/work/tao-te-ching">Tao Te Ching · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruins of Loulan and Niya were rediscovered by Sven Hedin and <b>Aurel Stein</b> around 1900.</blockquote><a href="/state/kroraina">Kroraina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pest-hungary">Pest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.5,19.1&amp;year=1914" aria-label="Pest on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4996 498 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aurel Stein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5446 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5446 768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.5,19.1&amp;year=1914">What was here in 1914? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
